<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Dacia</h1><p class="st-meta">82 BCE – 106 CE (approx.) · capital Sarmizegetusa Regia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom of the Dacians in the Carpathian basin, united by Burebista and defended by Decebalus against Trajan, whose conquest created Roman Dacia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>About 82 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a> united the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> and Dacian tribes, aided by the high priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deceneus">Deceneus</a>. He built a realm from the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, defeating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/boii">Boii</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/scordisci">Scordisci</a> and taking Greek coastal cities such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/olbia-pontica">Olbia</a> and Apollonia.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The heart of the kingdom lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orastie-mountains">Orăștie Mountains</a>, where fortresses and sanctuaries ringed the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmizegetusa-regia">Sarmizegetusa Regia</a>. Kings ruled with a priestly elite devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalmoxis">Zalmoxis</a>. Gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mining, iron working and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> supported the state. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista's</a> murder in 44 BCE the realm split, but it was again a strong kingdom under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decebalus">Decebalus</a>, whose forces destroyed a Roman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelius-fuscus">Cornelius Fuscus</a> in 86 or 87 CE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan's</a> two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-dacian-wars">Dacian Wars</a> (101–102 and 105–106 CE) destroyed the kingdom; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmizegetusa-regia">Sarmizegetusa</a> fell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decebalus">Decebalus</a> killed himself in 106 CE. Most of the land became the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>, commemorated on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-column">Trajan's Column</a>, and Dacian identity later became central to Romanian national history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-dacia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Dacia" width="320" height="233"><figcaption>Image: Portasa Cristian · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dacia_during_Burebista_REMAKE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>82 BCE – 106 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sarmizegetusa Regia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Burebista, Scorilo, Decebalus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dacian polytheism (cult of Zalmoxis)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dacian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Romania, Moldova, Serbia, Bulgaria, Hungary</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dacian Kingdom, Kingdom of Burebista, Kingdom of Decebalus</dd></dl></aside></div>
